A||||15||artherd||I Sure Do Love General Motors!||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||10-24-2001||03:46 AM||||Well, I found the source of the high-speed shake, and low-speed klunking today...<P>The ENTIRE front end of my Formula needs to be redone.  Every wear item is shot (and yes, even the ()#@$&* caliper slider pins are siezed again, unreliable pieces of crap!)<P>Did I mention the car has only 71,000miles?<P>The good news: I can pull the suspension off my older formula (which is undergoing an engine swap, so she won't miss the parts.)  You see, I *ALSO* *JUST* replace everything wear-items on that car....<P>Anyway, just needed to vent.  Pulled all the suspension off the t-top today, got to pull the silver car's, clean it a bit, and slap it in tommorow...<P>Fun fun fun! (Thank GOD for air tools!)<P>Best!<BR>Ben. "3 fieros, *none* running" Cannon.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||rodmcneill||10-24-2001||04:07 AM||||What is it about Fiero front ends, low grade parts or just the design? Most other cars I have had needed very little front end work done.||||reg||5
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||10-24-2001||11:33 AM||||On GM's it seems some things just start falling apart at certan miles. My family has had all GM cars for as long as I can remember. GMs never got much over 100k. This is true with most american cars probably. <P>Fieros are built well, so I dont think they have this problum as much though.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Gerald||10-24-2001||11:55 AM||||What would be the best replacment parts to use? Im gonna redo my whole car this winter.<P>------------------<BR>87 coupe 5 spd||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||10-24-2001||12:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B>On GM's it seems some things just start falling apart at certan miles. My family has had all GM cars for as long as I can remember. GMs never got much over 100k. This is true with most american cars probably. <P>Fieros are built well, so I dont think they have this problum as much though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In my experience, with reasonable maintenance, GM cars typically last well past 100k miles without major problems. The Fiero isn't built any better or worse than any other GM product from the same era.<P>We don't have enough background on how Ben's Formula has spent it's 13 years and 71,000 miles of life to draw any accurate conclusions. Even if it just sat around most of the time, lubricants break down and seal deteriorate.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spektrum-87GT||10-24-2001||05:33 PM||||if its not a fiero, i wont own a gm ever. my family had a lot... they've all been terrible. Not to mention they're hard to work on compared to fords and chryslers. they're engineering is some of the poorest in the automobile industry(everything from they're egr tubes to they're 3.4 dohc belt setup) i cant name all of the times i've found pieces of various metal and plastic that served no other purpose other than to get in the way. one thing i've gotta say, gm engines last a long time if properly maintained... its the other parts that crap out. The build quality of gm's is pathetic(then again, so are most chryslers and fords) gm... in a word... pathetic.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Master_Sushi||10-24-2001||06:21 PM||||Fieros seem way better build then most GM cars. Just look at the interiour, no big gaps in the dash trim, real screws holding in the trim plates (I like this others might not), not much is glued or snaped on inside. I had the inside of my 84 coupe COMPLETELY riped out. After puting it all back together I don't have one squeek or rattle. How many GM's can you say that about? <BR>I drive a 99 firebird once and a while. The thing makes more strange noises and rattles inside then my 17 year old fiero!||||reg||1
Z||000007||BN Boomer||10-24-2001||09:35 PM||||Geez, maybe you guys should quit while you are ahead just get rid of your Fieros now. Go buy a Honda and come back and tell us how they or any other import are so much easier to work on. Having done major work on both a Honda and a Subaru, I can honestly tell you that I would rather work on just about any GM product. There is almost always more space under the hood and far fewer electrical and vacuum devices to worry about. I often wonder how small the typical Asian's hand must be to cram so much stuff in there and still be able to get tools in for assembly and disassembly.<P>Another huge benefit and smart move on GM's part, in my opinion, is the commonality of their components and designs. I really like the fact that so many different parts from so many years can be swapped around with relative ease and once you've figured out the basics of GM designs, it seems easy to work on just about any of their vehicles. I don't find their to be a big learning curve when jumping from a Fiero to a Sunfire to a Suburban. Or maybe I just a higher than average mechanical inclination.<P>Don't forget that the Fiero is really nothing special among GM products. They used off the shelf drivetrains and components similar to other GM vehicles. <P>We have a 94 Suburban with 154,000 miles and no major problems, a 96 Sunfire with 103,000 miles and I haven't even changed the original spark plugs yet, and just bought a 2000 Impala with the Series II 3800 that should last us for a long time as well. And then there's the 85 Fiero GT with a 2.8 that gave up the ghost at about 110,000 miles and is living on with a transplanted 88 2.8 with about 90,000 on the clock now. I did recently come across a SC3800 II from a 2000 Pontiac complete with cradle, trans, harness, ECM, and halfshafts for $2600 OBO. Hmmmmm. I wonder how that would fit into a Honda....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>GM vehicles have typically been very good to me and my extended family. Like I said, the Fiero wasn't built any better or worse than any other GM product from the same era.<P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.gmemd.com/locomotives/naf/images/sd70sm.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||10-25-2001||05:53 AM||||Oops, I didn't mean for this to turn into a GM bashing thread, and I'll say here for the record that I find the Fiero a genuine pleasure to work on!!!<P>Anyone who says it's hard hasn't touched any automobile made since 1990. Peroid.<P>That said, it's just the build/design quality is simply horrible...  I mean, at *seventy* k?!?!  (agreed, I don't know the car's history 66k and previous, asside from it's orgional owner being a Fiero collector and this his 'weekend car that he gets to DRIVE and not just look at' but still!)<P>Just found out my rack bushing is SHOT too.  I mean royally shot.  Hellooooo Rodney!<P>Now the *(&*( brakes are giving me crap (guess what? I've replaced ALL FOUR calipers' slider pins now!!!)   God, what I wouldn't give for rod-ends, and real 4-pot brakes....  soon little one, sooooooon.....<P>On the upshot, during the whole process, I decided to expiriment and.... cut the front springs...  just exactly one coil....  and it's really great! dosen't suck at all like I was expecting, wow.  I did this to a set of "ARD" front 88 springs.  Spring rate did increase, but just enough.   Ride height is now great and cool looking.  Handling will have to be assessed after an alightment.<P>But the ride *quality* went up.  that's the strange thing.  These cars continue to amaze me.<P>Dispite (maybe because, otherwise I wouldn't likely have been fuddling around under there) their little quirks!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||artherd||10-25-2001||05:57 AM||||Oops, I didn't mean for this to turn into a GM bashing thread, and I'll say here for the record that I find the Fiero a genuine pleasure to work on!!!<P>Anyone who says it's hard hasn't touched any automobile made since 1990. Peroid.<P>That said, it's just the build/design quality is simply horrible...  I mean, at *seventy* k?!?!  (agreed, I don't know the car's history 66k and previous, asside from it's orgional owner being a Fiero collector and this his 'weekend car that he gets to DRIVE and not just look at' but still!)<P>Just found out my rack bushing is SHOT too.  I mean royally shot.  Hellooooo Rodney!<P>Now the *(&*( brakes are giving me crap (guess what? I've replaced ALL FOUR calipers' slider pins now!!!)   God, what I wouldn't give for rod-ends, and real 4-pot brakes....  soon little one, sooooooon.....<P>On the upshot, during the whole process, I decided to expiriment and.... cut the front springs...  just exactly one coil....  and it's really great! dosen't suck at all like I was expecting, wow.  I did this to a set of "ARD" front 88 springs.  Spring rate did increase, but just enough.   Ride height is now great and cool looking.  Handling will have to be assessed after an alightment.<P>But the ride *quality* went up.  that's the strange thing.  These cars continue to amaze me.<P>Dispite (maybe because, otherwise I wouldn't likely have been fuddling around under there) their little quirks!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: anybody wanna buy a 2.8 V6 with only 72k Miles?  WITH A (#$&@(&$ ROD-KNOCK ALREADY?!?!?<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||Cozmo||10-25-2001||07:23 AM||||I'm getting ready to address this "loosey-goosey" front end thing myself on my Targa. Ben, could you let us know what parts you use and any tricks or tips you have to do the job easier?<BR>I agree with Greg, one of the best things about GM is their parts and design commonality.<BR><P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||10-25-2001||10:31 AM||||GM's quality was a lot better in the 80's than the 1990's.  What happened was GM fell into the same trap Chrysler had, and Ford recently has gone down the same road.<P>When GM was in financial troubles circa 1990-1992, they began "telling" their suppliers that they would sell the same parts to GM for a cheaper price.  To accomplish this and stay in business, the suppliers had to sacrafice quality of the parts to make them cheaper to produce.  Hence, lower quality parts.  My family has had 3 Fiero's all have been very reliable IMO.  Of the three, we have only replaced one waterpump, one alternator, two starters and a fuel pump(same car, a re-build crapped out).  No other major components.  All these went onto my fathers 87 Coupe, and all but the starter were done when the car had over 100,000 miles and the car was at least 10 years old.  By comparson, my parents had a 92 Trans Sport which had all that replaced plus the heater core and windshield wiper motor with under 60K miles and was 6 years old or younger!  My mom now has a 97 Buick Century, which has 24K miles.  The surface flaked off the brake rotors already, the power steering tube crapped out where the rubber is permanently fitted to the metal portion of the tube, and its leaking oil out of a few engine gaskets already.  I'd expect better for such a low milage car.  Hopefully they are doing something to reduce this trend, because every model year just gets worse.<P>It makes you wonder if dropping $20-$30 on a new car is worth it anymore.  I'll keep my Fiero!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackStang95||10-25-2001||02:37 PM||||I think all of the big 3 have their quirks that annoy some people.  The bottom line is when you drive cars aggressively things will break, 70k miles is hardly a spring chicken, and its prolly more the age than the mileage.  Japanese cars technically have fewer defects, and if that's your definition of quality they are the cars of choice.  If you like cars that aren't cookie cutter and have a soul they'll never replace American muscle.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||SLYGUY||10-25-2001||03:20 PM||||I never thought that I would own an 80s era domestic because I've never heard anything good about their quality or reliability. In 1993 I had a 86 SEV6 and loved it. That set the little car into my soul and I never looked back. <BR>Since then I've had many Japanese cars but deep down there was a little voice reminding me of that little Fiero. Scrimped and saved and as you can tell by the picture I am totally enjoying having this little car back in my life.<BR>Quirky okay. Tempermental, yeah. Needy, not more than other GMs. Heart and soul of a pure red blooded American....HELL YEAH!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||GTFiero1||10-25-2001||04:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>if its not a fiero, i wont own a gm ever. my family had a lot... they've all been terrible. Not to mention they're hard to work on compared to fords and chryslers. they're engineering is some of the poorest in the automobile industry(everything from they're egr tubes to they're 3.4 dohc belt setup) i cant name all of the times i've found pieces of various metal and plastic that served no other purpose other than to get in the way. one thing i've gotta say, gm engines last a long time if properly maintained... its the other parts that crap out. The build quality of gm's is pathetic(then again, so are most chryslers and fords) gm... in a word... pathetic.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>man you guys have bad luck with your gm cars...we've only owned gm cars and they've all been great as long as its been maintained. we had a caprice that had close to 200,000mi . GM cars are not harder to work on. where did you get this from?! GM are usually the easiest, i have a friend who owns a ford ranger and he likes ford but he says "ford, important parts in stupid places" and my autoshop teacher always says gm cars are easier to work on, all the mechnic shops near me have mechanics that own GM cars. the caprices are over built to got 300,000+ miles, well at least the 9C1's. your complaining about tubes and belts? on the new ford ranger the oil filter is directly over the starter, its a pain in the @$$. and the jeep cherokee's, you have to loosen the oil filter on the bottom, then do the rest from the top. i have found that gm oil filters are conveinently located. yeah, maybe the previous owner of this formula didnt keep up on maintenence, maybe the previous owner changed the oil him self and never gresed the grese fittings or anthing...you never know...stop busting on GM products, we've had all GM cars for a reason, we wouldnt buy just gm cars if their quality sucked and we've owned a lot of GM cars 100,000+ miles, our cadillac had to have the front end redone. it has 70,000mi but sat most its life as it was used only a few months a year in the summer while it was down in florida all its life until now<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||10-26-2001||01:39 AM||||Guess what I just found?<P>Lower balljoints are shot too.<P>Frankly, I'd almost rather have a japanese car...<BR>ONLY if it's an NSX though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> and at that, the newer 3.2T model.<P>I'd expect this kind of crap at over 150k, but *70!?* IMHO, that IS new for a car.<P>PS: I am replacing all bushings just with Poly for now.    this suspension will only be in here another year or so, then it's up to tubular components and rod ends with huge brakes and real wheels  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   That is, once I get the Silver car going...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
